  "text": "A boy carried a flute onto my boat. He kept it tucked beneath his arm while the other passengers found their seats. Whenever someone passed, he turned a little to keep it out of the way.\n\nThe Empty Bench. Thank you for bringing it, Pip. I’ll read it after the last boat returns. I’ve been carrying my own page about for so long; it’s good to have one from her.\n\nHe hesitated, then lifted it. His fingers found their places. The first notes were so quiet that I could hardly hear them over the water. I stayed where I was and listened.\n\n“Are you waiting for someone?” I asked. He shook his head. One wing of the bird was torn. Its tip hung lower than the other, and his thumb rested just below the tear.\n\nI reached toward the bird. He pulled it closer. My hand stopped between us. For a moment, neither of us said anything. Water tapped against the side of the boat.\n\nWhen he stepped onto the dock, he carried the bird carefully in both hands. At the last step he paused, then found a clear place for his foot.\n\n“Would you like some help?” I asked. “We could look at it together.” He hesitated. Then he held out the bird, with both hands underneath it.\n\n“Now I can tell my grandmother how we fixed it,” he said. He tilted the bird once, very slightly, and the mended wing caught the light.\n\n“I have an obligation to the passengers. Someone may need a hand with that last step. I can’t go while they’re still on the boat.”\n\nI’d like that. After a day at the dock, I’d like to sit beside Grandma and listen for once. You have my permission to read it.\n\n“Would you like to play something?” I asked. He looked down at the flute, then along the boat. “You don’t have to,” I added.\n\nI’ll still be at the dock, then. Take my page and read it for me. If Grandma shares one of hers, would you bring me a copy?",
